</div>

<div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
    <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">Hempstead UFSD Leadership Directory</h1>
    <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in Hempstead UFSD, Hempstead, Nassau County.</p>

    <div class="contact-grid">

        <div class="contact-box">
            <h3>Special Education Office</h3>
            <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 District Office:</strong><br>Hempstead UFSD</div>
            <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:516-434-4092">516-434-4092</a></div>
            <div class="contact-item"><strong>📍 Mailing Address:</strong><br>185 Peninsula Boulevard, Hempstead, NY 11550</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
        <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYSED Special Education QA (SEQA).
    </div>


    <section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
        </h2>

        <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">

            <div class="protocol-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>.
                </p>
                <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary.</li>
                    <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em></li>
                    <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates and names.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="protocol-card">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder:
                </p>
                <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call.</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal).</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent.</li>
                    <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="advocacy-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-bullhorn"></i> Parent Advocacy Tips for Hempstead UFSD
        </h2>

        <div class="advocacy-content">
            <p style="font-size: 1.0rem; color: #444;">
                Navigating the special education system in Hempstead UFSD can be challenging. Here are some strategies, rooted in New York State regulations (Part 200) and the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA), to effectively advocate for your child:
            </p>

            <ol style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>
                    <strong>Know Your Rights:</strong> Familiarize yourself with the rights afforded to you and your child under IDEA and New York State Education Law. Pay particular attention to Part 200 of the Regulations of the Commissioner of Education, which outlines the specifics of special education in NYS. <a href="http://www.nysed.gov/specialed/laws-regulations" target="_blank">Review NYSED Regulations</a>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Request Evaluations in Writing (and be specific):</strong> If you believe your child needs special education services, make a formal, written request for an evaluation.  Be SPECIFIC. Don't just ask for a "special education evaluation." Request specific evaluations, such as a psychological evaluation, educational evaluation, speech and language evaluation, occupational therapy evaluation, and/or a functional behavioral assessment (FBA). This triggers the district's obligation to conduct a comprehensive evaluation within 60 school days of receiving your consent (8 NYCRR §200.4(b)(1)(i)).  Include the phrase "Pursuant to 8 NYCRR §200.4(b)(1)(i)" in your request to show you know the law.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Attend CSE Meetings Prepared:</strong>  Committee on Special Education (CSE) meetings are crucial. Before the meeting, review all evaluation reports, your child's IEP (if applicable), and prepare a list of questions and concerns. Bring any relevant documentation, such as independent evaluations or work samples. Under IDEA, you have the right to participate meaningfully in these meetings.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Document Everything:</strong> Maintain a detailed record of all communications with the school district, including emails, letters, and phone calls. Keep copies of all evaluations, IEPs, and other relevant documents. This documentation can be invaluable if you need to escalate your concerns.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Consider an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE):</strong> If you disagree with the district's evaluation, you have the right to request an IEE at public expense (8 NYCRR §200.5(g)). The district must either pay for the IEE or initiate a due process hearing to demonstrate that its evaluation was appropriate.  This is a powerful tool to get a second opinion.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Focus on Measurable IEP Goals:</strong> Ensure that your child's IEP contains specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, and time-bound (SMART) goals. Vague goals like "improve reading comprehension" are difficult to track and assess. Instead, aim for goals like "Increase reading comprehension from a 3rd-grade level to a 4th-grade level as measured by the [Name of Assessment] by June 2024."
                </li>
                 <li>
                    <strong>Request Specific Programs and Services:</strong> Advocate for the specific programs and services your child needs, such as specialized reading instruction (e.g., Orton-Gillingham), speech therapy, occupational therapy, counseling, or assistive technology. Don't be afraid to name specific programs that have a proven track record.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Understand the Continuum of Services:</strong> New York State requires a continuum of services, ranging from general education with supplementary aids and services to more restrictive settings like special classes or residential placements (8 NYCRR §200.6). Ensure that your child is placed in the Least Restrictive Environment (LRE) that meets their needs.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Request a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA) and Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP):</strong> If your child's behavior is interfering with their learning or the learning of others, request an FBA to identify the triggers and functions of the behavior. The FBA should then inform the development of a BIP with strategies to address the behavior.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Don't Be Afraid to Ask Questions:</strong> If you don't understand something, ask for clarification. The CSE team should be able to explain the evaluation results, IEP goals, and services in a way that you understand.
                </li>
                <li>
                     <strong>Consider Mediation or Due Process:</strong> If you are unable to resolve your concerns with the CSE team, you have the right to request mediation or file a due process complaint. Mediation is a voluntary process that can help you and the district reach an agreement. Due process is a formal legal proceeding that can result in a binding decision.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Connect with Parent Support Groups:</strong>  Connect with other parents of children with disabilities in Hempstead UFSD. Sharing experiences and resources can provide valuable support and guidance. Consider joining local or statewide parent organizations.
                </li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="common-pitfalls-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-exclamation-triangle"></i> Common Pitfalls in Hempstead UFSD
        </h2>

        <div class="pitfalls-content">
            <p style="font-size: 1.0rem; color: #444;">
                Based on anecdotal evidence and interactions with families in Nassau County, here are some common pitfalls to be aware of when working with Hempstead UFSD's special education department:
            </p>

            <ul style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>
                    <strong>Delayed Evaluations:</strong>  Districts sometimes delay evaluations beyond the legally mandated 60-day timeline.  To avoid this, send all requests in writing (certified mail, return receipt requested, is ideal).  If the timeline is missed, immediately file a complaint with NYSED.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Insufficient IEP Goals:</strong>  IEPs may contain vague or generic goals that do not adequately address the student's specific needs.  Scrutinize the goals carefully and advocate for more specific and measurable objectives.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Resistance to IEEs:</strong> Districts may be reluctant to approve IEEs or may attempt to unduly influence the choice of evaluator.  Know your rights and insist on your right to an IEE if you disagree with the district's evaluation.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Lack of Qualified Staff:</strong> Some districts struggle to provide adequately trained or certified special education teachers and related service providers. Ask about the qualifications of the staff working with your child.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Placement Issues:</strong> Ensure the IEP drives the placement decision, not the other way around. Districts sometimes try to fit students into pre-existing programs rather than creating individualized programs tailored to their needs. Demand data to support any placement recommendation.
                </li>
                 <li>
                    <strong>Inadequate Transition Planning:</strong> Transition planning (preparing students for life after high school) should begin no later than age 15 (8 NYCRR §200.4(d)(2)(i)(c)). Some districts fail to provide adequate transition services.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
        <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
            <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="faq-content">
            <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">What is the difference between a CSE and a CPSE?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    The Committee on Special Education (CSE) is responsible for students aged 5-21 who are suspected of having a disability. The Committee on Preschool Special Education (CPSE) serves children aged 3-5.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">How do I request an IEP meeting?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    Submit a written request to the CSE chairperson or your child's special education teacher. Be specific about the purpose of the meeting (e.g., to review IEP goals, discuss progress, or address concerns).
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">What if I disagree with the IEP?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    You have several options, including requesting mediation, filing a State Complaint with NYSED, or requesting a due process hearing. Document your concerns in writing and follow the escalation protocol outlined above. You can also implement "stay put" which means the current IEP stays in place until the dispute is resolved (8 NYCRR §200.5(k)).
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">Can I bring an advocate to CSE meetings?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    Yes, you have the right to bring an advocate, attorney, or other support person to any CSE meeting.  (IDEA, 20 U.S.C. § 1415(c)(1)(C)). It is highly recommended to bring an advocate if you anticipate a contentious meeting.
                </p>
            </div>

              <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
                <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333;">What is a 504 plan?</h3>
                <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                    A 504 plan provides accommodations for students with disabilities who do not require specialized instruction but need support to access the general education curriculum. Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973 is a federal civil rights law that prohibits discrimination based on disability. Students with 504 plans do not have IEPs and are not considered "special education" students, but they are entitled to accommodations.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
</div>
</main>
